  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is agriculture a good career in India? Yes — but only once you separate two very different bets hiding inside the same word.</strong>
    A degree-holder agriculture career (research, extension, agribusiness, agri-input sales, or agritech) is a
    salaried or business-professional path with real government routes, a genuinely growing agribusiness and
    agritech layer, and honest competition at the top exams. Full-time land-owning farming is a completely different
    economic reality tied to landholding size, monsoon risk, and crop prices at harvest — and most Indian farm
    households operate less than 2 hectares of land. Building a real <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> —
    a subject specialisation, a business or data layer, and visible proof of fieldwork — is what turns an
    agriculture degree into genuine <strong>high income opportunities</strong>, not the word "agriculture" on a
    resume by itself.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Agriculture as a degree-holder career (research, extension, agribusiness, agri-input, agritech) and agriculture as full-time farming are two different economic realities — answer "which agriculture" before answering "is it good."</li>
      <li>Government routes are real but genuinely competitive: the ICAR Scientist (ARS) exam has only a few hundred posts nationally per cycle against a large applicant pool.</li>
      <li>Agribusiness and agritech currently offer some of the sector's strongest pay ceilings, but agritech specifically carries real startup-style funding risk — several known agritech companies restructured during the 2023-2024 funding slowdown.</li>
      <li>About 86% of Indian farm households operate less than 2 hectares of land, and roughly half of India's net sown area is still rainfed — full-time farming income depends heavily on landholding size and monsoon timing, not just effort or skill.</li>
      <li><strong>A subject specialisation plus a business, data, or agri-fintech layer is what actually separates a strong agriculture career from a commodity BSc Agriculture degree.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your own fit with one real fieldwork or internship proof point before committing years to an exam-heavy government route or an expensive private agribusiness institute.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

