  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is agriculture a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Agriculture is a real, structurally growing field in India — but "is agriculture good" is really three or four
    different questions wearing one coat. A government ICAR scientist, an agribusiness manager at an FMCG company,
    an agritech product manager, and a farmer cultivating two acres of wheat are all "in agriculture." Their income
    structure, risk, and daily work have almost nothing in common.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most articles on this keyword answer the question as if it only means one of these paths, usually the
    degree-holder version, and quietly skip the farming-as-livelihood reality entirely. That gap is exactly where
    people end up disappointed — either because they picked a degree expecting it to lead back to the family farm,
    or because they picked farming expecting the income stability of a salaried job.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Both the "agritech is the next big thing" pitch and the "agriculture means a farmer's hard, uncertain life"
      warning are incomplete on their own. The degree-holder side of this field has real, growing, well-paid tracks.
      The farming side carries real structural risk tied to land size and weather that no amount of individual
      hustle fully removes. Treat them as separate decisions, not one blended verdict.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="two-worlds">Two very different bets, one word</h2>

  <p>
    Before comparing pay tables or exam routes, get this distinction straight, because it changes which section of
    this article actually applies to you.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
