        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither side is automatically the "real" or "better" version of agriculture. A degree-holder career does not
      make someone less connected to agriculture, and farming is not a fallback for people who "couldn't get a
      proper job." They are simply different economic structures, and this article treats them as such instead of
      blending them into one soft, feel-good answer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="degree-paths">The BSc Agriculture and ICAR route</h2>

  <p>
    Most degree-holder agriculture careers in India start with a 4-year BSc Agriculture (Honours), earned either
    through the ICAR All India Entrance Examination for Admission (AIEEA UG) — which reserves a share of seats
    across ICAR-linked and state agricultural universities nationally — or through a specific state's own CET route
    into its state agricultural university.
  </p>

  <p>
    From there, the degree branches into genuinely different careers depending on what comes next: an MSc and the
    ARS/ICAR Scientist exam for research, a state PSC exam for government extension work, an MBA or PGDM for
    agribusiness, or a direct move into private agri-input or agritech company roles. The degree is a starting
    point, not a single fixed destination — treat the choice after the BSc as seriously as the choice to do the BSc
    in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2 id="govt-track">Government and PSU agriculture jobs</h2>

  <p>
    Government routes remain the most sought-after destination for many agriculture graduates, partly for genuine
    mission fit and partly for job security. They are also genuinely competitive, and not equally accessible.
  </p>

  <div class="employer-grid">
    {govtRoutes.map((item) => (
