      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Agritech's growth story is real, and so is its funding-cycle risk. Several well-known agritech companies
      restructured, cut headcount, or shut specific business units during the broader startup funding slowdown of
      2023-2024. Evaluate any specific employer's recent funding round and headcount trend directly — do not treat
      "agritech is booming" as a guarantee about one particular company's stability.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="private-agri-input">Private agri-input companies</h2>

  <p>
    Seed, fertiliser, and crop-protection corporates — companies like Bayer Crop Science, Syngenta India, UPL,
    Corteva Agriscience, PI Industries, Rallis India, and Mahyco — are large, established employers that hire
    agriculture graduates directly, mainly into sales, agronomy-support, and R&amp;D functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Territory Sales Officer and field-agronomist roles are the most common entry point and involve real travel and
    farmer-facing work, closer in daily texture to extension work than to a desk job. Research and plant-breeding
    roles pay meaningfully more but typically require an MSc or PhD, not just a BSc — check a specific role's
    qualification requirement before assuming a bachelor's degree is enough for the higher-paying research track.
  </p>

  <h2 id="farming-reality">The farming reality: land, MSP, and monsoon</h2>

  <p>
    This is the section most "is agriculture a good career" articles either skip or romanticise, and it deserves
    the same honest treatment as the degree-holder tracks above.
  </p>

  <p>
    According to NSSO Situation Assessment Survey data, the average monthly income of an agricultural household in
    India sits roughly in the ₹10,000-13,000 range, and that figure varies enormously with landholding size. About
    86% of Indian farm households operate less than 2 hectares of land, per the Agriculture Census — a structural
    ceiling on income that no amount of individual effort fully overcomes without more land, higher-value crops, or
    strong market linkages.
  </p>

  <p>
    MSP (Minimum Support Price) is real government policy, but it functions as a safety floor mainly for a handful
    of crops — chiefly wheat and paddy in certain states — and most farmers across most crops still sell primarily
    into the open market, where prices swing with supply, demand, and quality at harvest time. Roughly half of
    India's net sown area remains rainfed rather than irrigated, which keeps monsoon timing a direct, unhedged risk
    to household income in a way no salaried career carries.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      None of this means farming cannot work. Larger landholders, horticulture and cash-crop growers, and farmers
      with strong direct market linkages or Farmer Producer Organisation (FPO) membership can and do earn
      meaningfully more than the national averages suggest. It means the honest starting point for anyone
      considering full-time farming as a livelihood is land size, crop economics, and irrigation access — not a
      general belief that "farming is always needed, so it will always pay."
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Genuine agrarian distress in parts of rural India is also real and well-documented in government and
    third-party data, alongside the genuine AgTech growth story in agribusiness and agritech. Both are true about
    Indian agriculture at the same time, describing different layers of the same broad sector — treat any article
    that tells only one half of this story with caution.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-agritech">AI and the future of agriculture work</h2>

  <p>
